WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.303 --> 00:00:02.120
(upbeat digital music)

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.970
- [Narrator] This isn't
any ordinary gamer.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.070
- [Nolan] I had the reputation at the time

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.490
of this mad genius.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.760
- [Narrator] He created
a bunch of companies

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.120
focusing on robots …

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.490
- [Nolan] Automobile navigation.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:16.980
- [Narrator] Microwave components.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.960
- [Nolan] And a bunch of others.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.518
- He is the man who launched
the video game revolution.

00:00:22.518 --> 00:00:24.210
(upbeat digital music)

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.820
- [Narrator] But there's
one more creation you may

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.260
not know about …

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.260
A place where a kid can be a kid.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.440
- [Announcer] Let's welcome
creative genius, Nolan Bushnell.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.011
- [Broadcaster] Nolan Bushnell.

00:00:35.011 --> 00:00:38.670
(upbeat digital music)

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.810
- [Narrator] In many ways,
you can trace the history

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:45.210
of video games all the way back
to this man, Nolan Bushnell.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.700
In 1972, Nolan co-founded one of the first

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.210
video game companies.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.580
They called it Atari.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.440
Their first hit is a table
tennis game called, Pong.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.840
It sets record sales
and permanently changed

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:58.690
the arcade world.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.060
Even though arcades were
hitting critical mass,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.440
families were still
hesitant to accept them

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.240
into their lives.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.390
To change hearts and
minds, Nolan knew that

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.340
arcades needed a new and friendly face.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.060
This is the story of how
he introduced the world

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.952
to a new mascot of
gaming, Chuck E. Cheese.

00:01:16.952 --> 00:01:20.202
(upbeat digital music)

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.440
In the 1970s and ’80s, this is
what a video game looked like.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.210
Yeah, it doesn't look like much.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.770
But back then, games
like these turned Atari

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:32.436
into a multi billion dollar company.

00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:35.103
(digital music)

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.550
- At Atari, we had explosive growth.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.860
We were just printing money
because we were lean and mean,

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.680
and had good games.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.690
- [Narrator] But having
good games wasn't enough.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:52.110
The market was saturated,
and Atari needed to break

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.433
into a new demographic.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.100
- [Nolan] I wanted to create
a chain of big arcades

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.590
focusing on kids.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.300
- They've even passed laws
restricting the use of the games.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.430
- But, there were certain
cities that didn't

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.170
wanna have arcades
because teenagers came and

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.530
they'd cause trouble.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.210
So I felt that disguising
it as a restaurant was

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.100
a good business strategy.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.842
Chuck E. Cheese was an arcade,
masquerading as a restaurant.

00:02:18.842 --> 00:02:21.092
(laughing)

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.190
- [Narrator] Though Nolan had
the idea, he needed someone

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.793
to turn it into a reality.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.870
So he tapped one of the brightest

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.010
business minds within Atari.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.240
A man as ambitious as he was.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.230
A man as crazy as he was.
- Come on, baby.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.859
Meet the first president of
Chuck E. Cheese, Gene Landrum.

00:02:41.859 --> 00:02:44.442
(upbeat music)

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.823
- I have been pretty competitive in my life.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.860
I was 20 years old, I was
Mr. Ft. Lauderdale.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.040
I won a hundred tennis tournaments.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.550
So, I did business kind of the same way.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.900
I happened to be an aggressive
guy, and go get it done,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.176
and win the game.

00:03:02.176 --> 00:03:04.690
(upbeat music)

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.980
When Nolan hired me to do this,
I actually looked at myself

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.520
and said, “What do you
know about food, Gene?”

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.980
Nothing.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.080
- [Narrator] But that was a
risk Nolan wanted to take.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.200
He knew that creating something unorthodox

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.233
required visionaries, not
experts set in their ways.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.910
- Experts don't understand the future.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.150
Too many times you have
to un-teach experts.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:32.370
So, when it came to spinning
up the Chuck E. Cheese project,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.370
I thought Gene would be the perfect guy.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.030
- [Narrator] So, where did Gene start?

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.503
Well, with food.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.340
- [Gene] Pizza makes sense
because families love pizza.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.350
And, guess what?

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.353
It takes 20 minutes to make a pizza.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.010
So, while they're waiting,
the kids are playing

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.693
the games, and I had Chuck
actually deliver the pizza.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.870
And I went down south
and found a guy who did

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.740
costumes for Disney.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.570
I said, “Look I don't want a mouse.”

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.020
Disney's got Mickey.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.990
And I don't want a rat, it’s too hard core.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.740
I want a soft rat.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.650
And he did it.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.510
- [Narrator] And after
only three months of work,

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.320
Chuck E. Cheese Pizza Time
Theater opened its doors

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.232
for the first time on May 17, 1977.

00:04:19.232 --> 00:04:22.300
(upbeat music)

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.870
- Everybody was willing to say that

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.570
talking robots in a pizza
parlor with a lot of games

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.053
was a really stupid idea.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:34.310
And in fact, the day we
opened I knew we'd screwed up.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:35.950
The place wasn't big enough.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.010
- My joint was filled.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.310
There wasn't a seat.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.360
And once I learned how to do
it, I opened a store a week.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.310
Fifty stores I opened in one year!

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.800
- I had no fear.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.483
I knew that it was gonna do well.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.600
(subtle dramatic music)

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.750
- [Narrator] Chuck E. Cheese's
initial stores were a hit.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.220
But at the parent company
Atari, things were going

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:01.270
a little differently.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.590
- Warner bought Atari, and all of a sudden

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.040
you had a whole bunch of
New York suits pruning

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.463
my pet projects.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.690
I was really upset about
that, and it dawned on me

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:19.480
that I was having a
lot more fun working on

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.133
Chuck E. Cheese than on Atari.

00:05:23.274 --> 00:05:26.240
- [Narrator] And so Nolan
eventually left Atari.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.980
Over time, the company
struggled and faded away

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.283
as the spotlight turned to new
gaming giants like Nintendo.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.630
But while kids today may
not know the name Atari,

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:39.483
Chuck E. Cheese is still
a childhood hot spot.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.380
After 40 years, the buck
tooth, pizza-serving

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.110
stuffed rat still stands as a testament to

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:49.914
Nolan's innovation in
the video game industry.

00:05:49.914 --> 00:05:52.430
(subtle dramatic music)

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:53.570
- It was a whirlwind.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.623
It was more successful than
I thought it would ever be.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.320
But, Chuck E. Cheese, it’s a viable concept.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.400
It makes sense.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.050
It still makes sense.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.053
- Chuck E. Cheese's is better than ever.

00:06:05.053 --> 00:06:05.890
Thank you, thank you, thank you.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:10.130
- There's a vanishingly
small number of places

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.310
where parents and children
can have a fun night out.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:16.600
And that was one of the goals.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:18.420
You know, I feel good about that.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.490
I saw it, I did it.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:21.640
I was at the beginning.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:23.583
Enough.

00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:25.812
(laughing)

00:06:27.895 --> 00:06:29.812
(ding)

